# Whether the arrow pointer reads on every background (vtium/vtium#102).
# vtium draws the pointer as an arrow. #102 would draw it by inverting the
# pixels under it instead, so it reads on any background. This guide shows
# backgrounds where an arrow might be hard to see; the answers decide
# whether #102 is needed.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=pointer

title: the arrow pointer on every background

step: White, black and mid gray
run: clear; for c in 107 40 100; do for i in $(seq 1 8); do printf "\033[${c}m%*s\033[0m\n" "$(tput cols)" ""; done; done; sleep 30
expect: three bands fill the screen: white, black and mid gray. Move the mouse
expect: across all three. The arrow is easy to see on each band, and its tip
expect: is clear enough to aim at a single character.

step: Strong colors
run: clear; for c in 41 42 43 44 45 46; do for i in $(seq 1 4); do printf "\033[${c}m%*s\033[0m\n" "$(tput cols)" ""; done; done; sleep 30
expect: six bands: red, green, yellow, blue, magenta and cyan. Move the mouse
expect: across every band. The arrow is easy to see on each one.

step: Busy text
run: clear; for i in $(seq 1 24); do printf '\033[3%dm%s\033[0m\n' $((i % 8)) "$(head -c 400 /dev/urandom | base64 -w0 | cut -c1-$(tput cols))"; done; sleep 30
expect: the screen is full of dense colored characters. Move the mouse over
expect: the text. You can follow the arrow without losing it.
